{
  "subtask_id": 6,
  "findings": "Synthesis under a linked-object, activity-label attribution: orders are linked to items (orders→items, 7659 links), and items are linked to packages through packages→items (7659 links), so order-level decomposition must be inferred via linked items/packages rather than direct order→package links. Using median activity-level waiting+sojourn times on the item trajectory, core item-handling contributes approximately 176804 s per item for normal picking (pick item waiting 84271 s + sojourn 92533 s). If stock exceptions are included in expectation, item out of stock and reorder item add about 110944 s weighted by their item frequency, yielding an approximate item-handling contribution of 287748 s. Packaging/delivery-labelled activities on the item trajectory contribute approximately 888085 s for create package + send package + package delivered, or about 906000 s if failed-delivery delay is frequency-weighted in. This gives an approximate split of 24% item-handling versus 76% packaging/delivery among the labelled item/package activity delays. For non-stock-exception items, the split is closer to 17% item-handling versus 83% packaging/delivery; for stock-exception cases, item-handling can rise to roughly 45% of the item/package-labelled delay. The dominant labelled delays are package delivered sojourn on items (median 607303 s), payment reminder/order-payment delays, and stock-exception/reorder delays for affected items; on package objects, failed delivery is the main slow-package discriminator. However, this decomposition depends strongly on the assumption that activity-level delays are additive and attributable by label. In particular, the very large package delivered sojourn on items may actually include downstream wait until later order/payment events, not pure delivery execution time. Package-object lifecycle medians are much smaller: median package flow time for median packages is 101706.5 s, and slow packages are mainly characterized by failed delivery. Therefore, reducing item-handling time alone would meaningfully shift overall flow time only under restrictive assumptions: if item-handling delays are on the critical path, if saved item time is not absorbed by downstream packaging/delivery/payment waiting, and especially for the subset with item out of stock/reorder. For the overall order population, evidence suggests limited impact: order flow is weakly related to item count (Pearson r=0.0079, Spearman rho=0.0131), package flow is weakly related to package item count (Pearson r=-0.0062, Spearman rho=0.0114), slow orders are dominated by payment reminder behavior, and slow packages by failed delivery. Thus, unless packaging/delivery and payment/reminder bottlenecks are also reduced, item-handling improvements would likely produce only a modest shift in observed overall order flow time.",
